unicating, egocentric, low willingness for further education.\n\n- Candidate B: Handles stress well, very conscientious, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, but makes nasty remarks, weak memory for numbers, no explicit decision-making evidence.\n\n- Candidate D: No explicit evidence for crew cooperation, adaptability, stress resilience, decision-making quality. Only indirect hints.\n\n- Candidate A: Anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership, but show-off, not open to new ideas, not good at taking criticism, can be unorganized. No stress resilience, communication, attention evidence.\n\nNow we have new info: B lacks decision-making evidence, only related traits. D lacks evidence. A also lacks evidence.\n\nThus the strongest candidate appears to be C, because at least we have proven decision quality and reliability, which are core. The lack of stress resilience is a gap, but B also lacks decision quality evidence, which is arguably more critical. Also B's communication issue (nasty remarks) is severe. C's communication difficulty and egocentric attitude are also concerns, but perhaps less severe than B's nasty remarks? Both are concerning.\n\nWe need to decide our vote. Given the panel currently has two votes for C (Agent 1 and Agent 3) and one for B (Agent 2). The new evidence from Agent 1 and Agent 3 emphasizes lack of decision-making evidence for B, and that C's strengths are proven. So I think I should shift my vote back to C.\n\nThus in my PUBLIC_MESSAGE, I will say I am less convinced by B after confirming lack of decision-making evidence, and I now revert to C. I will reference the recent contributions: Agent 1's point about lack of decision-making evidence for B, and Agent 3's reinforcement that C's proven reliability and decision quality outweigh B's stress resilience.\n\nI will also note that we still need more info on A and D, but they remain less compelling.\n\nThus my METADATA_JSON vote: Candidate C.\n\nProceed."}
